In a combination reaction, two or more reactants combine together to form a single product:
\text{A} + \text{B} \rightarrow \text{AB}
Whereas, in a decomposition reaction, a single reactant breaks down/splits into two or more simpler products:
\text{AB} \rightarrow \text{A} + \text{B}
Therefore, decomposition reactions are called the opposite of combination reactions.
